1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:02,700
How Do 3D Printers Work?

2
00:00:02,650 --> 00:00:11,337
Three-dimensional printing, formally additive manufacturing, constructs objects by depositing material layer by layer from a digital model.

3
00:00:11,337 --> 00:00:20,875
The process begins with a computer-aided design file, which slicing software divides into horizontal cross-sections typically 20 to 200 micrometers thick.

4
00:00:20,875 --> 00:00:41,500
Unlike subtractive manufacturing (cutting from solid blocks) or formative manufacturing (molding), additive processes produce minimal waste — often below 5% material loss — and enable geometries impossible with conventional techniques: internal lattice structures, conformal cooling channels, and topology-optimized components.

5
00:00:41,500 --> 00:01:02,075
Fused deposition modeling (FDM), patented by Scott Crump in 1989 and the most widespread technique, feeds thermoplastic filament — commonly acrylonitrile butadiene styrene (ABS), polylactic acid (PLA), or polycarbonate — through a heated nozzle at 190 to 260 degrees Celsius.

6
00:01:02,075 --> 00:01:10,537
The nozzle deposits molten material along computer-defined toolpaths in the X-Y plane, then the build platform descends by one layer height.

7
00:01:10,537 --> 00:01:20,000
FDM produces anisotropic parts; interlayer adhesion is weaker than in-plane strength, typically 50-80% of bulk material properties.

8
00:01:20,000 --> 00:01:30,462
Stereolithography (SLA), patented by Charles Hull in 1984, uses an ultraviolet laser to selectively cure liquid photopolymer resin in a vat.

9
00:01:30,462 --> 00:01:37,737
Each layer is traced by the laser, polymerizing the resin through photoinitiation, then the build platform shifts.

10
00:01:37,737 --> 00:01:49,737
SLA achieves layer thicknesses as small as 25 micrometers, making it suitable for dental models, hearing aid shells, jewelry casting patterns, and microfluidic devices.

11
00:01:49,737 --> 00:01:57,912
Digital light processing (DLP) cures entire layers simultaneously using a projected image, accelerating production.

12
00:01:57,912 --> 00:02:11,750
Industrial techniques include selective laser sintering (SLS), which fuses polymer or metal powder with a laser, and electron beam melting (EBM), which uses an electron beam in vacuum for titanium and cobalt-chrome alloys.

13
00:02:11,750 --> 00:02:18,050
These methods produce fully dense metal parts for aerospace engine components and orthopedic implants.

14
00:02:18,050 --> 00:02:30,175
Medical bioprinting deposits living cells in hydrogel scaffolds; researchers have printed vascularized cardiac patches and cartilage constructs, though functional transplantable organs remain distant.

15
00:02:30,175 --> 00:02:38,787
The global additive manufacturing market exceeded 15 billion dollars in 2023 and continues to grow at roughly 20% annually.

